    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_claude` and all tests pass (`pytest -q`: 2 passed, including the existing `fetch_status` test).\n\n**The installed SDK can't take the service's HTTP client.** anthropic 1.8.0 is built on `httpx2`, not `httpx`, and raises a `TypeError` if it's given an `httpx.Client` or `httpx.Timeout`. So `build_http_client()` can't be passed in directly. The stub transport your tests pass also needs to be an `httpx2` transport, such as `httpx2.MockTransport`. `httpx2` was already installed as a dependency of the SDK, so nothing was added.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **`app/http.py`**: I added `egress_client_options(http_lib=httpx, transport=None)`. It builds the timeout, the `X-Egress-Tag` header and the `log_egress` hook from the existing values, using whichever library it's given (`httpx` or `httpx2`). `build_http_client` now uses it and behaves exactly as before.\n- **`app/claude.py`**: `build_claude` builds an `httpx2` client from those same options and passes it to `Anthropic(api_key=..., max_retries=2, timeout=..., http_client=...)`. The timeout is also given to `Anthropic` itself. Without that, `messages.create` would use the SDK's own 600-second read timeout on every call instead of 60 seconds.\n- **`tests/test_claude.py`** (new): the stub transport fails twice with 529 and then succeeds. The test checks that all 3 attempts carry the header and each gets exactly one \"app.egress\" log line. It also checks the 5s connect and 60s read timeouts, `max_retries == 2`, and the stub transport receives the requests.\n\nI also considered making plain `import httpx` load `httpx2` for the whole process. I didn't do it: it's global, it has to run before anything imports `httpx`, and it would affect `fetch_status`."
  },
